// ============================================================
// Process: deriving initial + target (D, C) from a user's stated intent
// Tags used in comments:
//   [REASONING]  -> judgment call / creative choice, not computed
//   [COMPUTED]   -> deterministic math, follows a fixed formula
//   [VERIFIED]   -> ran real code to check a claim, not trusted by eye
//
// Real imports. Everything tagged [COMPUTED] reads one of these rather than
// restating a constant in a comment, so the pseudocode cannot drift from the
// kernel the way a copied "0.3" silently would.
//
//   import {
//     classifyAttractor, lyapunov,
//     LYAPUNOV_ALPHA, STABILITY_THRESHOLD,
//     VOID_D_THRESHOLD, VOID_C_THRESHOLD,
//     suggestTransitionOperators, attractorPenalty,
//   } from "./phasePortrait.js";
//   import { solve, distance, DISTANCE_THRESHOLD } from "./solver.js";
//   import { allOperators, operatorMeaning, operatorClass } from "./formalism.js";
//   import { STABLE_TARGET_DISSIPATION } from "../engine/core.js";
//
// VOID_D_THRESHOLD / VOID_C_THRESHOLD are not re-exported by kernel/index.ts;
// they come from phasePortrait.js directly.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// The one rule this whole file exists to enforce
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// A free-text intent must NEVER be read directly into a (D, C) pair. Reading
// "I keep rewriting the same function" and emitting `{ D: 0.85, C: 0.75 }` is
// inventing a measurement: the numbers carry four significant figures of
// authority that the reading cannot support, and nothing downstream can tell
// them apart from numbers that were derived.
//
// The intent is read into SIGNALS — things a reader can point at in the
// transcript and disagree with — and a FIXED FORMULA turns signals into
// numbers. The judgment stays visible and arguable; the arithmetic stays
// checkable. Same discipline engine/core.ts already applies in
// `deriveInitialDissipation`.
// ============================================================

function deriveStateFromIntent(intentText, transcript):

    // Step 1 — read the intent into signals, NOT into numbers
    signals = {
        uncertainty:           readHowUnsureTheyAre(intentText),      // 0..1
        failedChecks:          listThingsThatDemonstrablyFailed(transcript),
        unresolvedClaims:      listAssertionsNothingHasSettled(transcript),
        contradictionDetected: didTheyStateTwoIncompatibleThings(intentText),
    }
    // [REASONING] every field here is a reading of English. But each one is a
    // reading a reviewer can CHALLENGE by name — "that check didn't fail",
    // "that claim was resolved in the previous turn" — which is the entire
    // difference between this and picking D = 0.85 by feel.
    //
    // Note the asymmetry: `uncertainty` is the only free scalar, and it is the
    // one to be most suspicious of. The other three are counts of nameable
    // things. Prefer moving weight onto the countable fields.

    // Step 2 — signals become numbers by formula, not by judgment
    // D = clamp01(0.2 + uncertainty * 0.4 + failedChecks.length * 0.1)
    // C = clamp01(0.15 + unresolvedClaims.length * 0.08 + (contradiction ? 0.25 : 0))
    initialDC = deriveInitialDissipation(signals)
    // [COMPUTED] the formula is fixed. [REASONING] belongs to its CONSTANTS,
    // not to this call — engine/core.ts:405 says so in its own docstring:
    // "Authored constants, not measured." The 0.4 weight on uncertainty is a
    // choice nobody has calibrated. This step launders no judgment; it just
    // pins it to one auditable place instead of re-making it per intent.
    //
    // BLOCKER: `deriveInitialDissipation` is currently module-private in
    // src/engine/core.ts (line 407) — it has no export. Wiring this for real
    // means exporting it, or moving it somewhere the kernel can reach without
    // the kernel importing from engine/. Do not copy the formula; a second
    // copy is the thing this file is trying to prevent.

    initialLabel = classifyAttractor(initialDC.D, initialDC.C)   // real import
    // [COMPUTED] and [VERIFIED] in the same breath — unlike the template
    // process this replaced, the initial label is now DERIVED, never guessed,
    // so there is nothing here for Step 7 to catch. Half the old file's
    // guess-then-check loop is gone because the guess is gone.


function chooseTargetArc(intentText, initialLabel):

    // Step 3 — only the DESTINATION is authorial now.
    targetLabel = readWhereThisShouldLand(intentText)
    // [REASONING] irreducible: "they want to stop thrashing and ship" is a
    // reading. But it is a choice among THREE values, not among a continuum of
    // (D, C) pairs — a judgment call with three outcomes is one a reviewer can
    // actually check.

    // Default, when the intent does not say: the engine already has an opinion.
    if targetLabel is unstated:
        targetDC    = STABLE_TARGET_DISSIPATION          // real import, engine/core.ts
        targetLabel = classifyAttractor(targetDC.D, targetDC.C)
        // [VERIFIED] STABLE_TARGET_DISSIPATION is { D: 0.1, C: 0.1 };
        // lyapunov gives V = 0.14, so it classifies "J=0". Every planning path
        // in engine/core.ts already aims here. Reusing it means an
        // intent-derived arc and an engine-planned one target the same point
        // instead of quietly disagreeing.

    // Step 3b — is this arc one the formalism can actually tell?
    suggested = suggestTransitionOperators(initialLabel, targetLabel)   // real import
    // [VERIFIED] enumerated all nine pairs against the built kernel:
    // `suggested` is empty for exactly the three same-label arcs
    // (J=0→J=0, S*→S*, ∅→∅) and non-empty for all six cross-label ones.
    // diagnose.ts:42 suppresses the "Suggested operators:" line when empty.

    if suggested is empty:
        // Same-label arc. For a TEMPLATE this was a thin-output problem. For a
        // derived intent it is a real FINDING and must be reported as one:
        // "the state your intent describes is already the state you asked for."
        return { initialLabel, targetLabel, suggested, sameLabel: true }
        // [REASONING] do not re-roll the target to manufacture a non-empty arc.
        // That fabricates a problem to have something to solve.

    // Step 3c — direction sanity
    if attractorPenalty(targetLabel) > attractorPenalty(initialLabel):   // real import
        // [VERIFIED] penalties read from formalism.json: J=0 → 0.1,
        // S* → 0.3, ∅ → 1.0. So this fires on any arc toward collapse.
        flag("intent asks to move toward the costlier attractor")
        // [REASONING] a flag, not a rejection. The shipped `rigid` template
        // deliberately runs J=0 → S* uphill; some intents genuinely want more
        // agitation, not less.

    return { initialLabel, targetLabel, suggested, sameLabel: false }


function numbersForLabel(label, initialDC):

    // Step 4 — the target's numbers. Needed only when Step 3 chose a target
    // label other than the STABLE_TARGET_DISSIPATION default.
    //
    // The label→numbers rule, from classifyAttractor:
    //   V(D, C) = lyapunov(D, C) = D + LYAPUNOV_ALPHA * C
    //   if V < STABILITY_THRESHOLD:                         return "J=0"
    //   elif D > VOID_D_THRESHOLD or C > VOID_C_THRESHOLD:  return "∅"
    //   else:                                               return "S*"
    // [COMPUTED] fixed, read from the kernel. ALPHA and STABILITY_THRESHOLD
    // load from formalism.json, so a change there moves these bands under us —
    // which is why this solves the inequality instead of hardcoding a band.

    switch label:
        case "J=0":
            C = pickIn(0, STABILITY_THRESHOLD / LYAPUNOV_ALPHA)  // [REASONING] free
            ceilingD = STABILITY_THRESHOLD - LYAPUNOV_ALPHA * C  // [COMPUTED]
            D = ceilingD - margin                                // [REASONING] headroom
        case "∅":
            D = VOID_D_THRESHOLD + margin                        // [COMPUTED] band
            C = pickIn(0, 1)                                     // [REASONING] free
            // one axis over its threshold suffices; V >= STABILITY_THRESHOLD
            // then holds for free, since D > 0.8 already exceeds 0.3.
        case "S*":
            D = pickIn(STABILITY_THRESHOLD, VOID_D_THRESHOLD)    // [COMPUTED] band
            C = pickIn(0, VOID_C_THRESHOLD)                      // [REASONING] free

    // 4b — margin is not decoration. A point on a threshold classifies by float
    // comparison, and 0.3 - 0.4*0.5 is 0.09999999999999998.
    // [REASONING] margin >= 0.02 keeps the arc off that edge.

    // 4c — the arc must be big enough for the solver to have anything to do.
    if distance(initialDC, {D, C}) <= DISTANCE_THRESHOLD:        // real import
        // [VERIFIED] solve() returns at the start node the moment distance
        // <= DISTANCE_THRESHOLD (0.12): { D: 0.15, C: 0.13 } against the
        // stable target is 0.0583 away and returns SUCCESS with an EMPTY
        // sequence. A caller that prints "SUCCESS" there reports a solved plan
        // when the truth is "nothing to do".
        report("intent is already within the solver's success radius")
        // [REASONING] report it; do not push the target further out to
        // manufacture a sequence.

    return { D, C }


function describeIntent(intentText, arc):

    // Step 5 — the prose. Narrow the field first: a diagnosis naming an
    // operator irrelevant to the arc reads as authoritative and is not.
    relevant = arc.suggested                        // [COMPUTED] from Step 3b
    if relevant is empty: relevant = allOperators() // real import, formalism.ts

    for op in relevant:
        meaning = operatorMeaning(op)               // real import, formalism.ts
        cls     = operatorClass(op)                 // real import, formalism.ts
        score[op] = howWellThisPhraseDescribesTheIntent(intentText, meaning, cls)
    // [REASONING] pure pattern-match on English. "analysis paralysis" against
    // Meta's meaning string is a reading, not a similarity metric — do not
    // dress it up as one by reporting a number.

    text = phraseAsFailureMode(top(score, 1 or 2))
    // [REASONING] house style, matched by eye against problem_templates.json,
    // which names a failure mode rather than a prescription:
    //   "Meta ∘ Meta loop (infinite reflection)"      — too much of an operator
    //   "Excessive Ana without Kata (no compression)" — an unbalanced pair
    //   "In the Void (∅), need rescue operators"      — a location

    return text
    // UNVERIFIABLE as prose. The one claim inside it that IS checkable — that
    // the operators it names are ones the engine would reach for — is below.


function verifyDerivedArc(derived):

    // Step 6 — the label claims. Cheap, so there is no excuse for skipping it.
    assert classifyAttractor(derived.initial.D, derived.initial.C) == derived.initialLabel
    assert classifyAttractor(derived.target.D,  derived.target.C)  == derived.targetLabel
    // [VERIFIED] executes the real compiled kernel function. Nothing is
    // presented to the user as a label until this passes.

    // Step 7 — the arc claim.
    solution = solve({                              // real import, solver.ts
        initial: derived.initial,
        target:  derived.target,
        beamWidth: 10,                              // matches diagnose.ts:27
    })
    // [VERIFIED] runs the real A* beam search under the same options the CLI
    // uses, so the reported sequence is the one `lambda solve` would return.

    if solution.length == 0:
        report("no operators needed — initial already within DISTANCE_THRESHOLD")
        // [COMPUTED] see 4c. Distinguish this from a real plan in the OUTPUT;
        // `success` is true in both cases and the flag alone will mislead.

    if not solution.success:
        report("PARTIAL — the operator algebra cannot reach this target")
        // [COMPUTED] success means the final state came within
        // DISTANCE_THRESHOLD of the target. [VERIFIED] all five shipped
        // templates return SUCCESS at beamWidth 10, so a PARTIAL here is a
        // signal about the derived arc, not a known-flaky solver.

    for op in operatorsNamedIn(derived.diagnosis):
        if op not in solution.sequence:
            flag("diagnosis names " + op + ", solver never uses it")
            // [REASONING] a flag, not an auto-fix. [VERIFIED] `stuck` is a live
            // instance: its diagnosis names Meta, and solve() returns
            // Axis ∘ Telo ∘ Telo, containing no Meta. That template describes
            // the CAUSE, not the cure — so this must never be wired to rewrite
            // the text automatically.

    return solution


// ============================================================
// What the sweep found — read this before trusting a derived initial state
// ============================================================
// [VERIFIED] swept the deriveInitialDissipation formula across
// uncertainty 0..1 (step 0.05) x failedChecks 0..8 x unresolvedClaims 0..10 x
// contradiction {false, true} = 4158 signal combinations, classifying each with
// the real classifyAttractor:
//
//   J=0 :    3 combinations
//   S*  : 1646
//   ∅   : 2509
//
// J=0 requires failedChecks == 0, contradiction == false, AND
// 0.4*uncertainty + 0.032*unresolvedClaims < 0.04 — effectively "the user
// reported nothing at all". The formula's floor is D = 0.2, C = 0.15, giving
// V = 0.26 against a 0.3 threshold: there is only 0.04 of headroom before every
// intent classifies as agitated.
//
// Two consequences for anything built on this file:
//
//  1. A derived initial label is almost always S* or ∅, and both arcs into the
//     default J=0 target ARE mapped (S*→J=0: Kata, Telo, Seed, Latch;
//     ∅→J=0: Telo, Kata, Axis, Bind). So the common path works. The
//     same-label degenerate case in Step 3b is rare, not the norm.
//
//  2. ∅ is reachable from SEVEN unresolved claims plus a contradiction with
//     zero failed checks and zero uncertainty (D = 0.2, C = 0.96). ∅ carries a
//     penalty of 1.0 against S*'s 0.3 — a 3.3x cost swing that here turns on
//     how many sentences the reader chose to call "unresolved". Before shipping
//     this, either bound `unresolvedClaims` or re-weight its 0.08 coefficient.
//     Right now the most consequential number in the output is decided by a
//     count nobody has defined.
